## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). .NET on macOS delegates to these native libraries for cryptographic operations. Starting with .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS because there is no underlying OS implementation to call.

Windows and Linux are unaffected — Windows uses CNG and Linux uses OpenSSL, both of which still support DSA.

## How to fix it

**Best option — migrate off DSA entirely.** DSA is a legacy algorithm with limited key sizes. Replace with **ECDSA** or **RSA** for new signatures, and re-sign any artifacts you control.

**If you must verify existing DSA signatures**, use a managed implementation like [BouncyCastle](https://www.nuget.org/packages/BouncyCastle.Cryptography):

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

// Load the public key and verify with BouncyCastle
var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
signer.Init(false, publicKeyParams); // DsaPublicKeyParameters
signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**If you want a runtime guard** to keep `DSA.Create()` on platforms that support it and fall back otherwise:

```csharp
static bool IsDsaSupported()
{
    try { using var _ = DSA.Create(); return true; }
    catch (PlatformNotSupportedException) { return false; }
}
```

**TL;DR**: Apple dropped DSA → .NET 11 can't provide it on macOS. Migrate to ECDSA/RSA, or use BouncyCastle for legacy verification.
